Well, Marie, you may have forgotten. But in this city, there are laws about this kind of thing.

00:45:39.180 --> 00:45:44.130
I guess I'll just take my claim to court and see what they say.

00:45:44.180 --> 00:45:50.130
You can do what the hell you like. I ain't giving you a cent.

00:45:50.180 --> 00:45:54.130
Ah, now this may be someone who can help you change your mind.

00:45:54.180 --> 00:45:56.130
What did you say?

00:45:56.180 --> 00:46:00.180
There's someone who wants to talk to you. Open the door.

00:46:02.180 --> 00:46:03.130
Good morning.

00:46:03.180 --> 00:46:04.130
Can I help you?

00:46:04.180 --> 00:46:06.130
You must be in Mrs. Paris.

00:46:06.180 --> 00:46:10.130
My name is Marie LaVoe. Who are you?

00:46:10.180 --> 00:46:13.130
My name is George Andrews.

00:46:13.180 --> 00:46:16.130
How can I help you, Mr. Andrews?

00:46:16.180 --> 00:46:20.130
Well, I think my client, Mr. Paris, is here.

00:46:20.180 --> 00:46:21.130
You're what?

00:46:21.180 --> 00:46:28.130
My client. I'm an attorney at law. I'm advising Mr. Paris on his claim to have for your estate.

00:46:28.180 --> 00:46:32.180
Why? You son of a bitch.

00:46:36.180 --> 00:46:40.130
Get out! Both of you!

00:46:40.180 --> 00:46:43.130
Mrs. Paris, you're making a big mistake here.

00:46:43.180 --> 00:46:48.130
Don't you call me Mrs. Paris? My name is Marie LaVoe.

00:46:48.180 --> 00:46:50.130
Now, get out!

00:46:50.180 --> 00:46:58.130
Mrs. Paris, since the day of your marriage to Mr. Paris, he has been legally entitled to one half for your estate.

00:46:58.180 --> 00:47:01.130
Did you hear me? Get out!

00:47:01.180 --> 00:47:03.180
Mrs. Paris?

00:47:05.180 --> 00:47:09.130
I told you. Don't call me Mrs. Paris.

00:47:09.180 --> 00:47:12.130
Jack? That's it.

00:47:12.180 --> 00:47:17.130
I want you to leave now and take your fat friend with you.

00:47:17.180 --> 00:47:20.130
You can't get rid of the problem by throwing us out, Marie.

00:47:20.180 --> 00:47:22.130
You want to bet money on that?

00:47:22.180 --> 00:47:29.130
You can bet your life on it. Mrs. Paris, I will instruct my client to press charges against you.

00:47:29.180 --> 00:47:33.130
Keep talking if you want me to hit you again.

00:47:33.180 --> 00:47:35.130
Fine. Okay, just hold on there.

00:47:35.180 --> 00:47:38.130
Goodbye, gentlemen. Have a nice day.

00:47:38.180 --> 00:47:43.130
Marie, you better think again about what you're doing.

00:47:43.180 --> 00:47:47.130
The next time we meet we'll be in court.

00:47:47.180 --> 00:47:52.130
The next time we meet, Jack Paris, we'll be in hell.

00:47:52.180 --> 00:48:00.130
Marie noticed that Jack had left his hat, and she thought about opening the door and throwing it out onto the street.

00:48:00.180 --> 00:48:02.130
But she didn't.

00:48:02.180 --> 00:48:05.130
Well, by you.

00:48:05.180 --> 00:48:10.130
What do you think we should do with Jack?

00:48:10.180 --> 00:48:15.130
Because he's gonna have to die.

00:48:15.180 --> 00:48:20.130
And die very soon.

00:48:20.180 --> 00:48:25.130
She took a sharp knife and began to cut the hat into small pieces.

00:48:25.180 --> 00:48:33.180
Then she put the pieces into a bowl of water.

00:48:34.180 --> 00:48:38.130
There's a lake near New Orleans called Lake Pontchartrain.

00:48:38.180 --> 00:48:47.130
A few days later, early in the morning before it was light, two fishermen were out on the lake in a boat.

00:48:47.180 --> 00:48:50.130
Hey! Help me with this net!

00:48:50.180 --> 00:48:51.130
What's the problem?

00:48:51.180 --> 00:48:56.130
I don't know. We got something real heavy in it.

00:48:56.180 --> 00:48:59.130
Hey, what's the matter with you? You losing your strength?

00:48:59.180 --> 00:49:02.130
You can't pull in the net without me helping you?

00:49:02.180 --> 00:49:06.130
Yeah, let me help me with this net. It's too heavy.

00:49:06.180 --> 00:49:10.130
Hey, come on! What can be so heavy that you can't hold it in?

00:49:10.180 --> 00:49:13.130
Just shut up and help me.

00:49:13.180 --> 00:49:15.130
Okay, let's do it.

00:49:15.180 --> 00:49:18.130
Pull.

00:49:18.180 --> 00:49:20.130
Jesus.

00:49:20.180 --> 00:49:23.130
You're right. What the hell did we catch today?

00:49:23.180 --> 00:49:28.130
Oh, will you shut up and keep pulling?

00:49:28.180 --> 00:49:31.130
Okay, here it comes.

00:49:31.180 --> 00:49:34.130
Stop it! What's the matter?

00:49:34.180 --> 00:49:37.130
Look, in the net, is that?

00:49:37.180 --> 00:49:40.130
Is that someone's arm?

00:49:40.180 --> 00:49:41.130
Oh, my God!

00:49:41.180 --> 00:49:44.130
Jesus, Mary and Joseph, it's a man.

00:49:44.180 --> 00:49:47.130
Come on. Help me get him in the boat.

00:49:47.180 --> 00:49:49.130
In the boat? No way!

00:49:49.180 --> 00:49:53.130
What you talking about? We have to take him back to the show.

00:49:53.180 --> 00:49:57.130
I ain't going nowhere with a dead body in the boat.

00:49:57.180 --> 00:50:00.130
Come on, boy. What you thinking about?

00:50:00.180 --> 00:50:02.130
We can't leave his body here in the lake.

00:50:02.180 --> 00:50:03.130
We sure can.

00:50:03.180 --> 00:50:06.130
For pity's sake.

00:50:06.180 --> 00:50:08.130
What is it?

00:50:08.180 --> 00:50:13.130
I just saw his face. His eyes are staring at me.

00:50:13.180 --> 00:50:17.130
Push him back in the water. Let someone else find him.

00:50:17.180 --> 00:50:19.130
Okay.

00:50:19.180 --> 00:50:23.130
Jesus, man, that was terrible.

00:50:23.180 --> 00:50:28.130
His eyes were staring at me and his mouth was wide open.

00:50:28.180 --> 00:50:32.130
He looked like he was in fear of the devil when he died.

00:50:32.180 --> 00:50:36.130
Stop talking about it. I don't want to know. Let's get out of here.

00:50:36.180 --> 00:50:40.130
The worst thing is, I recognized him.

00:50:40.180 --> 00:50:41.130
You did?

00:50:41.180 --> 00:50:42.130
Yes.

00:50:42.180 --> 00:50:47.180
Who was it? His name? His Jack Paris.

00:50:48.180 --> 00:50:56.130
The next day, Marie visited one of her hairdressing clients, a woman called Margaret Salinger.

00:50:56.180 --> 00:51:02.130
She lived in a fancy house in Dauphin Street, and her husband was a businessman.

00:51:02.180 --> 00:51:08.130
They seemed to have a lot of money. Mrs. Salinger lied gossiping with Marie.

00:51:08.180 --> 00:51:14.130
You know something, Marie. What's that, Margaret?

00:51:14.180 --> 00:51:18.130
You have a talent that no one else in the world has.

00:51:18.180 --> 00:51:21.130
I do. And what talent is that?

00:51:21.180 --> 00:51:25.130
You're the only person in this city who can make my hair look good.

00:51:25.180 --> 00:51:28.130
Oh, come on. I'm nothing special.

00:51:28.180 --> 00:51:34.130
Not true. I do believe that without you, I would look like a witch.

00:51:34.180 --> 00:51:38.130
Margaret, stop it. You're a fine-looking woman.

00:51:38.180 --> 00:51:41.130
Well, I don't know about that.

00:51:41.180 --> 00:51:48.130
But have you seen my daughter Laverne recently? She just turned 19, and she's a real good-looking girl.

00:51:48.180 --> 00:51:53.130
I have, and you're right. She's a real beauty.

00:51:53.180 --> 00:51:59.130
Margaret, I want to... Oh, I'm sorry. I didn't realize there was someone with you.

00:51:59.180 --> 00:52:01.130
What do you want, Henry?

00:52:01.180 --> 00:52:05.130
Margaret, I need to speak to you privately for a moment.

00:52:05.180 --> 00:52:07.130
Marie is doing my hair. Can't it wait?

00:52:07.180 --> 00:52:09.130
No, I'm afraid it can't.

00:52:09.180 --> 00:52:13.130
Is it something so secret that my hairdresser can't hear it?

00:52:13.180 --> 00:52:14.130
Well...

00:52:14.180 --> 00:52:20.130
Whatever it is, I'm sure I won't understand it. And I'm sure I'll forget it the minute I hear it.

00:52:20.180 --> 00:52:25.130
Yes, but if you don't mind, I need to talk to my wife alone.

00:52:25.180 --> 00:52:29.130
Oh, well, Marie. I guess I better listen to him.

00:52:29.180 --> 00:52:34.130
Perhaps you would like some lemon tea. Okay? I'll come back later.

00:52:34.180 --> 00:52:38.180
Go down to the kitchen, Marie. Betsy will look after you.

00:52:40.180 --> 00:52:48.130
Marie, how you doing? Fine, Betsy.

00:52:48.180 --> 00:52:50.130
You want some iced tea? That would be fine.

00:52:50.180 --> 00:52:54.130
Marie, I was so sorry to hear about Jack.

00:52:54.180 --> 00:52:59.130
Yes, well, you know, we weren't living together anymore.

00:52:59.180 --> 00:53:05.130
Well, yeah, I did hear that. But did they ever find out what happened to him?

00:53:05.180 --> 00:53:12.130
Well, I just think he got drunk as a skunk and fell in the lake.

00:53:12.180 --> 00:53:17.130
Marie, that's a terrible thing to say.

00:53:17.180 --> 00:53:20.130
Betsy, can I? Oh, sorry.

00:53:20.180 --> 00:53:24.130
Oh, hi, Laverne. Laverne, you know Marie, don't you?

00:53:24.180 --> 00:53:27.130
Yes, indeed. Hello, Mrs. Paris.

00:53:27.180 --> 00:53:31.130
Laverne. How many times do I have to tell you? Call me Marie.

00:53:31.180 --> 00:53:37.130
Right. Okay. Marie, I'm sorry to hear about your husband.

00:53:37.180 --> 00:53:41.130
Thank you, Laverne. It must be so hard to lose someone you love.

00:53:41.180 --> 00:53:44.130
Yes, I guess it is.

00:53:44.180 --> 00:53:46.130
Young Laverne is in love. Ain't you Laverne?

00:53:46.180 --> 00:53:48.130
No, Betsy, stop it.

00:53:48.180 --> 00:53:50.130
Are you in love, Laverne?

00:53:50.180 --> 00:53:53.130
Well, yes, I am. Who's the lucky boy?

00:53:53.180 --> 00:53:59.130
His name is Tom Martin, and he works on one of the boats that sailed to the islands.

00:53:59.180 --> 00:54:03.130
You know, the sugar boats? He works on a boat.

00:54:03.180 --> 00:54:09.130
Yes. I know it doesn't sound like much of a job, but he loves the sea.

00:54:09.180 --> 00:54:12.130
He wants to be captain of his own ship one day.

00:54:12.180 --> 00:54:18.130
I guess that means he's way a lot. Yes, he is. But he's coming home soon.

00:54:18.180 --> 00:54:22.130
That's great, Laverne. I hope you have a fine time when he comes back.

00:54:22.180 --> 00:54:28.130
I sure will, if... If what, Laverne? Nothing.

00:54:28.180 --> 00:54:35.130
Well, the thing is, Dad wants me to marry someone who's going to make a lot of money,

00:54:35.180 --> 00:54:38.180
and Tom sure isn't going to do that.

00:54:45.180 --> 00:54:53.130
So, Henry, what is it that's so important it can't wait till I've had my air done?

00:54:53.180 --> 00:54:57.130
Margaret, there's something I have to tell you.

00:54:57.180 --> 00:55:01.130
What? What is it, Henry?

00:55:01.180 --> 00:55:05.130
Margaret, we are in deep trouble.

00:55:05.180 --> 00:55:07.130
Why? What's happened?

00:55:07.180 --> 00:55:10.130
I... I have lost a lot of money.

00:55:10.180 --> 00:55:15.130
How much? A lot. How much?

00:55:15.180 --> 00:55:17.130
Ten thousand dollars.

00:55:17.180 --> 00:55:25.130
Ten... You lost ten thousand dollars? I didn't know you had ten thousand dollars.

00:55:25.180 --> 00:55:29.130
I didn't. I borrowed the money, and then I lost every cent.

00:55:29.180 --> 00:55:31.130
But how?

00:55:31.180 --> 00:55:38.130
I went into a land deal with a man. A terrible man called George Andrews, the man who was here this afternoon.

00:55:38.180 --> 00:55:41.130
That ugly little fat guy?

00:55:41.180 --> 00:55:47.130
Yes. He's a lawyer. And he's a crook. He stole all the money.

00:55:47.180 --> 00:55:52.130
Okay, so he committed a crime. Go tell a sheriff.

00:55:52.180 --> 00:56:02.130
Do that. The deal wasn't completely legal. I didn't realize it at the time. I... I guess I didn't read the small print in the contract.

00:56:02.180 --> 00:56:08.130
Henry, what are we going to do? This house isn't even worth ten thousand dollars.

00:56:08.180 --> 00:56:11.130
There is... a solution.

00:56:11.180 --> 00:56:13.130
What's that?

00:56:13.180 --> 00:56:17.130
Andrews will pay me the money back and pay off all my other debts.

00:56:17.180 --> 00:56:19.130
And why would he do that?

00:56:19.180 --> 00:56:24.130
Well, first of all, he has agreed to buy the land we own out west of the city.

00:56:24.180 --> 00:56:28.130
That piece of land near the Achavelia swamp?

00:56:28.180 --> 00:56:29.130
Yes.

00:56:29.180 --> 00:56:36.130
He wants to buy that land for ten thousand dollars? That's great!

00:56:36.180 --> 00:56:40.130
Not exactly. There's something else that he wants.

00:56:40.180 --> 00:56:41.130
What?

00:56:41.180 --> 00:56:42.130
Well...

00:56:42.180 --> 00:56:46.130
Henry, what is it that he wants?

00:56:46.180 --> 00:56:49.130
This afternoon, he met Leverne.

00:56:49.180 --> 00:56:50.130
And?

00:56:50.180 --> 00:56:56.130
He... well, he has asked me for her hand in marriage.

00:56:56.180 --> 00:57:00.130
What? Henry, Leverne is nineteen.

00:57:00.180 --> 00:57:02.130
Well, yes.

00:57:02.180 --> 00:57:05.130
And he is old and fat and disgusting.

00:57:05.180 --> 00:57:11.130
Henry, I am not going to allow that horrible man to marry my daughter.

00:57:11.180 --> 00:57:15.130
Margaret, things aren't as simple as that.

00:57:15.180 --> 00:57:16.130
I'll explain.

00:57:16.180 --> 00:57:18.180
The thing is...

00:57:24.180 --> 00:57:26.130
Can I come in?

00:57:26.180 --> 00:57:27.130
Yes.

00:57:27.180 --> 00:57:30.130
Margaret, what's the matter?

00:57:30.180 --> 00:57:33.130
I just heard some terrible news.

00:57:33.180 --> 00:57:35.130
What? What's happened?

00:57:35.180 --> 00:57:38.130
Henry just told me that someone wants to marry Leverne.

00:57:38.180 --> 00:57:41.130
You mean the young man who works on the sugar boat?

00:57:41.180 --> 00:57:43.130
No, Marie. Not Tom.

00:57:43.180 --> 00:57:47.130
A horrible old man called George Andrews.

00:57:47.180 --> 00:57:51.130
George Andrews? Yes.

00:57:51.180 --> 00:57:53.130
I'm an attorney at law.

00:57:53.180 --> 00:57:57.130
I'm advising Mr. Paris on his claim to have for your estate.

00:57:57.180 --> 00:57:58.130
Why?

00:57:58.180 --> 00:58:01.180
You son of a bitch.

00:58:09.180 --> 00:58:12.130
Yes, he's a disgusting little man.

00:58:12.180 --> 00:58:15.130
I know George Andrews.

00:58:15.180 --> 00:58:17.130
You do? Yes.

00:58:17.180 --> 00:58:21.130
In that case, you will understand why I'm so upset.

00:58:21.180 --> 00:58:26.130
You can't let that happen, Margaret. You cannot let that happen.

00:58:26.180 --> 00:58:28.130
Do you think I want it to happen?

00:58:28.180 --> 00:58:30.130
If you don't want it, don't allow it.

00:58:30.180 --> 00:58:33.130
It isn't as easy as that, Marie.

00:58:33.180 --> 00:58:35.130
We're in deep trouble.

00:58:35.180 --> 00:58:39.130
Henry owes money to this man a lot of money.

00:58:39.180 --> 00:58:42.130
Andrews can ruin us if he wants to.

00:58:42.180 --> 00:58:43.130
How?

00:58:43.180 --> 00:58:47.130
When he asked Henry for permission to speak to Leverne about marriage,

00:58:47.180 --> 00:58:49.130
Henry said no.

00:58:49.180 --> 00:58:55.130
So Andrews asked him to think again, and he said if Henry still said no,

00:58:55.180 --> 00:58:59.130
he was going to ask for all the money that Henry owes him.

00:58:59.180 --> 00:59:01.130
How much does he owe him?

00:59:01.180 --> 00:59:03.130
$10,000.

00:59:03.180 --> 00:59:05.130
Oh, my God.

00:59:05.180 --> 00:59:08.130
If we have to pay him, we'll have nothing.

00:59:08.180 --> 00:59:11.130
No house, no business, nothing.

00:59:11.180 --> 00:59:13.130
We'll be out on the street.

00:59:13.180 --> 00:59:15.130
Oh, Margaret.

00:59:15.180 --> 00:59:17.130
But if we agree to the marriage,

00:59:17.180 --> 00:59:21.130
Andrews will pay off all Henry's other debts as well.

00:59:21.180 --> 00:59:26.130
And he will also buy a useless piece of land that we own out near the Atchifu lion swamp.

00:59:26.180 --> 00:59:27.130
I see.

00:59:27.180 --> 00:59:30.130
I wish I could kill that man.

00:59:30.180 --> 00:59:33.130
But, you know, that wouldn't help.

00:59:33.180 --> 00:59:36.130
He has three children by his first marriage.

00:59:36.180 --> 00:59:38.130
To be clear of the dead.

00:59:38.180 --> 00:59:41.130
I would have to kill all of them as well.

00:59:41.180 --> 00:59:44.130
You know what the best thing would be?

00:59:44.180 --> 00:59:46.130
What's that, Margaret?

00:59:46.180 --> 00:59:48.130
Have Leverne marry him.

00:59:48.180 --> 00:59:50.130
And then kill him.

00:59:50.180 --> 00:59:55.130
You know, that ain't such a bad idea.

00:59:55.180 --> 00:59:58.130
That's a crazy idea, Margaret, and you know it.

00:59:58.180 --> 00:59:59.130
Marie?

00:59:59.180 --> 01:00:00.130
Yes.

01:00:00.180 --> 01:00:03.180
Could you do that for me?

01:00:04.180 --> 01:00:05.130
What?

01:00:05.180 --> 01:00:07.130
Kill a man.

01:00:07.180 --> 01:00:09.130
Margaret, are you out of your mind?

01:00:09.180 --> 01:00:12.130
Don't be all surprised with me, Marie.

01:00:12.180 --> 01:00:14.130
I know about the things that you do.

01:00:14.180 --> 01:00:16.130
What are you talking about?

01:00:16.180 --> 01:00:19.130
What are you talking about?

01:00:19.180 --> 01:00:22.130
You know, darn well what I'm talking about, Marie.

01:00:22.180 --> 01:00:24.130
I know about you.

01:00:24.180 --> 01:00:28.130
And all of those voodoo ceremonies down in Congo Square.

01:00:28.180 --> 01:00:30.180
Marie?

01:00:31.180 --> 01:00:33.130
Are you listening to me?

01:00:33.180 --> 01:00:34.130
I'm listening.

01:00:34.180 --> 01:00:36.130
Will you help me?

01:00:36.180 --> 01:00:40.130
Margaret, I don't normally work for white people.

01:00:40.180 --> 01:00:41.130
You don't, huh?

01:00:41.180 --> 01:00:43.130
Well, you're going to start now.

01:00:43.180 --> 01:00:45.130
Okay, Margaret.

01:00:45.180 --> 01:00:47.130
I'll try to help.

01:00:47.180 --> 01:00:49.130
You know I'd do anything for you.

01:00:49.180 --> 01:00:50.130
And...

01:00:50.180 --> 01:00:51.130
And what?

01:00:51.180 --> 01:00:54.130
I just remembered something.

01:00:54.180 --> 01:00:59.180
Something that George Andrews said to me just before Jack died.

01:01:00.180 --> 01:01:05.130
Mrs. Paris, since the day of your marriage to Mr. Paris, he has been legally entitled

01:01:05.180 --> 01:01:09.130
to one half by your estate.

01:01:09.180 --> 01:01:11.130
I have an idea.

01:01:11.180 --> 01:01:16.130
I think I know what we can do.

01:01:16.180 --> 01:01:17.130
Mother!

01:01:17.180 --> 01:01:18.130
Laverne.

01:01:18.180 --> 01:01:21.130
Father just told me the most terrible thing.

01:01:21.180 --> 01:01:24.130
He wants me to marry Mr. Andrews.

01:01:24.180 --> 01:01:25.130
I know.

01:01:25.180 --> 01:01:27.130
I won't do it, Mother.

01:01:27.180 --> 01:01:31.130
I'd rather cut my own throat than let that man touch me.

01:01:31.180 --> 01:01:33.130
Laverne, calm down.

01:01:33.180 --> 01:01:34.130
Calm down.

01:01:34.180 --> 01:01:35.130
Calm down.

01:01:35.180 --> 01:01:38.130
Mother, I won't do it.

01:01:38.180 --> 01:01:43.130
I love you and I love Dad, but I will not marry that man.

01:01:43.180 --> 01:01:46.130
The very thought of it makes me sick.

01:01:46.180 --> 01:01:48.130
Laverne, please.

01:01:48.180 --> 01:01:50.130
Just stop for a moment and listen to Marie.

01:01:50.180 --> 01:01:52.130
Listen to Marie.

01:01:52.180 --> 01:01:53.130
What for?

01:01:53.180 --> 01:01:54.180
Laverne.

01:01:55.180 --> 01:01:58.130
Just sit down for a moment and let me talk to you.

01:01:58.180 --> 01:02:00.130
I can help you.

01:02:00.180 --> 01:02:01.130
How?

01:02:01.180 --> 01:02:03.130
What can you do?

01:02:03.180 --> 01:02:05.130
If you marry this man.

01:02:05.180 --> 01:02:06.130
Marie, stop it.

01:02:06.180 --> 01:02:08.130
I don't want to hear it.

01:02:08.180 --> 01:02:09.130
Laverne.

01:02:09.180 --> 01:02:12.130
Quit squawking and hear me out.

01:02:12.180 --> 01:02:14.130
Sorry.

01:02:14.180 --> 01:02:16.130
In fact, it is Laverne.

01:02:16.180 --> 01:02:18.130
You only have to marry him.

01:02:18.180 --> 01:02:23.130
Go through with the wedding and you will immediately inherit half of his estate.

01:02:23.180 --> 01:02:24.130
And?

01:02:24.180 --> 01:02:25.130
And then will.

01:02:25.180 --> 01:02:27.130
I'll tell Marie.

01:02:27.180 --> 01:02:31.130
If you don't marry Andrews, we will lose everything.

01:02:31.180 --> 01:02:35.130
The house, the business, everything.

01:02:35.180 --> 01:02:38.130
Why didn't anyone tell me?

01:02:38.180 --> 01:02:41.130
Laverne, I didn't know about it until today either.

01:02:41.180 --> 01:02:44.130
Well, you two, listen to my plan.

01:02:44.180 --> 01:02:45.130
Sorry.

01:02:45.180 --> 01:02:47.130
Laverne.

01:02:47.180 --> 01:02:55.130
As soon as you are married to Mr. Andrews, I will make sure that, something happens to him.

01:02:55.180 --> 01:02:56.130
What?

01:02:56.180 --> 01:02:59.130
What's going to happen to him?

01:02:59.180 --> 01:03:01.130
Trust me, Laverne.

01:03:01.180 --> 01:03:04.130
He won't be a problem for you.

01:03:04.180 --> 01:03:06.130
But I need your help.

01:03:06.180 --> 01:03:11.130
You must go to Mr. Andrews' house and you must agree to marry him.

01:03:11.180 --> 01:03:14.130
Marie, I can't do that.

01:03:14.180 --> 01:03:16.130
I rarely can't.

01:03:16.180 --> 01:03:17.130
Hear me out.

01:03:17.180 --> 01:03:19.130
Agree to marry him.

01:03:19.180 --> 01:03:23.130
And bring me back something that belongs to him.

01:03:23.180 --> 01:03:25.130
What kind of thing?

01:03:25.180 --> 01:03:28.130
The simplest thing would be a handkerchief.

01:03:28.180 --> 01:03:31.130
Look, Margaret, I have to go now.

01:03:31.180 --> 01:03:33.130
But I'll be back later.

01:03:33.180 --> 01:03:35.130
Trust me, Laverne.

01:03:35.180 --> 01:03:38.130
Everything will be okay.

01:03:38.180 --> 01:03:41.130
Mama, why am I agreeing to this?

01:03:41.180 --> 01:03:45.130
I can't bear the idea of that man touching me.

01:03:45.180 --> 01:03:49.130
I deliver and trust Marie.

01:03:49.180 --> 01:03:51.130
She knows what she's doing.

01:03:51.180 --> 01:03:53.130
You'd better be right.

01:03:53.180 --> 01:03:59.180
Because if I have to live with that man, I'm going to kill myself.

01:04:01.180 --> 01:04:08.130
The next morning, Henry Salinger and his daughter Laverne visited George Andrews.

01:04:08.180 --> 01:04:11.130
Henry, hello George.

01:04:11.180 --> 01:04:13.130
So good to see you.

01:04:13.180 --> 01:04:14.130
Laverne.

01:04:14.180 --> 01:04:16.130
Hello, Mr. Andrews.

01:04:16.180 --> 01:04:21.130
Boy, Laverne, you look good enough to eat.

01:04:21.180 --> 01:04:23.130
Can we come in, George?

01:04:23.180 --> 01:04:25.130
A short thing, Henry.

01:04:25.180 --> 01:04:26.130
Come in.

01:04:26.180 --> 01:04:30.130
George, we've come to talk about your proposal.

01:04:30.180 --> 01:04:31.130
Good.

01:04:31.180 --> 01:04:34.130
And I hope you agree it's a good idea.

01:04:34.180 --> 01:04:38.180
Because if you haven't, then it's the end of the road for the Salinger's.

01:04:39.180 --> 01:04:43.130
Well, yes, Laverne has agreed to marry you.

01:04:43.180 --> 01:04:46.130
What did you do, Laverne?

01:04:46.180 --> 01:04:49.130
You just made the best decision of your life.

01:04:49.180 --> 01:04:50.130
Come here.

01:04:50.180 --> 01:04:51.130
Oh, Mr. Andrews.

01:04:51.180 --> 01:04:55.130
George, please, can we just discuss a few details?

01:04:55.180 --> 01:04:57.130
It's okay, Laverne.

01:04:57.180 --> 01:05:00.130
I can wait, but not for long.

01:05:00.180 --> 01:05:04.180
Let me get you folks a drink to celebrate.

01:05:05.180 --> 01:05:08.130
George, it's 11 o'clock in the morning.

01:05:08.180 --> 01:05:11.130
Isn't it kind of early to be drinking?

01:05:11.180 --> 01:05:13.130
Not when there's something like this to celebrate.

01:05:13.180 --> 01:05:14.130
No, George.

01:05:14.180 --> 01:05:15.130
I think...

01:05:15.180 --> 01:05:16.130
Oh, no, Dad.

01:05:16.180 --> 01:05:18.130
I think that's a nice idea.

01:05:18.180 --> 01:05:22.130
My father would like to join you, Mr. Andrews.

01:05:22.180 --> 01:05:23.130
Good.

01:05:23.180 --> 01:05:26.130
Well, I'm glad she has more sense than you, Henry.

01:05:26.180 --> 01:05:28.130
Even if she is just a woman.

01:05:28.180 --> 01:05:30.130
Hey, hold on there.

01:05:30.180 --> 01:05:33.180
And I'll be back with some bourbon.

01:05:34.180 --> 01:05:37.130
Laverne, what are you thinking about?

01:05:37.180 --> 01:05:38.130
Don't worry, Dad.

01:05:38.180 --> 01:05:39.130
I know what I'm doing.

01:05:39.180 --> 01:05:41.130
You know that I don't drink in the morning.

01:05:41.180 --> 01:05:43.130
Well, you're gonna have to start, Dad.

01:05:43.180 --> 01:05:47.130
I want you to accept a glass of bourbon from Mr. Andrews.

01:05:47.180 --> 01:05:48.130
And then...

01:05:48.180 --> 01:05:49.130
Here we are.

01:05:49.180 --> 01:05:51.130
A bottle of my best bourbon.

01:05:51.180 --> 01:05:53.130
It's 15 years old.

01:05:53.180 --> 01:05:57.130
Henry, have you any idea how much this stuff costs?

01:05:57.180 --> 01:05:58.130
No, George.

01:05:58.180 --> 01:06:00.130
I don't know how much it costs.

01:06:00.180 --> 01:06:02.180
Don't you want to know how much it costs?

01:06:03.180 --> 01:06:06.130
How much does it cost, George?

01:06:06.180 --> 01:06:09.130
This beauty is $7 a bottle.

01:06:09.180 --> 01:06:12.130
$7, but worth every cent.

01:06:12.180 --> 01:06:15.130
I have about 60 bottles of it in the cellar.

01:06:15.180 --> 01:06:18.130
Can I see the bottle, Mr. Andrews?

01:06:18.180 --> 01:06:20.130
Oh, why, yes, Laverne.

01:06:20.180 --> 01:06:21.130
Thank you.

01:06:21.180 --> 01:06:26.130
Well, Laverne, I didn't think you would be a bourbon drinker.

01:06:26.180 --> 01:06:28.130
Let me pour you a glass.

01:06:28.180 --> 01:06:29.130
Oh, no.

01:06:29.180 --> 01:06:31.130
I don't want to drink it, Mr. Andrews.

01:06:31.180 --> 01:06:35.130
I'm just interested in... in bottles.

01:06:35.180 --> 01:06:36.130
Oh!

01:06:36.180 --> 01:06:37.180
Be careful.

01:06:39.180 --> 01:06:40.130
Laverne.

01:06:40.180 --> 01:06:42.130
Oh, my God.

01:06:42.180 --> 01:06:44.130
That table is worth a fortune.

01:06:44.180 --> 01:06:47.130
I'm so sorry, Mr. Andrews.

01:06:47.180 --> 01:06:49.130
Let me clear up the glass.

01:06:49.180 --> 01:06:51.130
Don't be ridiculous, Laverne.

01:06:51.180 --> 01:06:54.130
I have servants to do this kind of stuff.

01:06:54.180 --> 01:06:55.130
Martha!

01:06:55.180 --> 01:06:56.130
No, no.

01:06:56.180 --> 01:06:57.130
I broke it.

01:06:57.180 --> 01:06:59.130
I want to clear it up.

01:06:59.180 --> 01:07:00.130
No, no, Laverne.

01:07:00.180 --> 01:07:01.130
Stop it.

01:07:01.180 --> 01:07:02.130
Get up off the floor.

01:07:02.180 --> 01:07:04.130
It's okay, Mr. Andrews.

01:07:04.180 --> 01:07:06.130
Here, take this piece.

01:07:06.180 --> 01:07:07.130
Gah!

01:07:07.180 --> 01:07:09.130
Laverne, careful.

01:07:09.180 --> 01:07:11.130
You just cut Mr. Andrews' hand.

01:07:11.180 --> 01:07:14.130
Jesus Christ, I'm bleeding to death.

01:07:14.180 --> 01:07:15.130
Martha!

01:07:15.180 --> 01:07:16.130
Oh, no.

01:07:16.180 --> 01:07:18.130
I'm so sorry.

01:07:18.180 --> 01:07:20.130
Quick, let me help you.

01:07:20.180 --> 01:07:24.130
Sit down here and give me a handkerchief.

01:07:24.180 --> 01:07:25.130
I...

01:07:25.180 --> 01:07:28.130
I'll use this one in your top pocket.

01:07:28.180 --> 01:07:30.130
It's okay, Dad.

01:07:30.180 --> 01:07:32.130
Mr. Andrews won't mind.

01:07:32.180 --> 01:07:34.130
Martha, where are you?

01:07:34.180 --> 01:07:36.130
I'm right here, Mr. Andrews.

01:07:36.180 --> 01:07:37.130
Oh, my goodness.

01:07:37.180 --> 01:07:39.130
What have you done to your hand?

01:07:39.180 --> 01:07:41.130
What have I done to my hand?

01:07:41.180 --> 01:07:43.130
You can see with your own eyes.

01:07:43.180 --> 01:07:45.130
I'm bleeding to death.

01:07:45.180 --> 01:07:47.130
Come with me into the pantry, Mr. Andrews.

01:07:47.180 --> 01:07:49.130
I'll clean that up.

01:07:49.180 --> 01:07:53.180
We'll come back later, George, when things have calmed down a little.

01:07:59.180 --> 01:08:05.130
Later that day, Laverne took Mr. Andrews' silk handkerchief to Marie's house.

01:08:05.180 --> 01:08:07.130
I got this, Marie.

01:08:07.180 --> 01:08:09.130
Well done, Laverne.

01:08:09.180 --> 01:08:11.130
How come it's covered in blood?

01:08:11.180 --> 01:08:16.130
I dropped a seven dollar bottle of bourbon on a glass coffee table.

01:08:16.180 --> 01:08:18.130
There was glass everywhere.

01:08:18.180 --> 01:08:24.130
Then I accidentally cut Mr. Andrews' hand with a piece of broken glass.

01:08:24.180 --> 01:08:25.130
My.

01:08:25.180 --> 01:08:27.180
I really enjoyed it.

01:08:28.180 --> 01:08:29.130
Oh, my God.

01:08:29.180 --> 01:08:30.130
What am I saying?

01:08:30.180 --> 01:08:31.130
I understand.

01:08:31.180 --> 01:08:34.130
What are you going to do with it?

01:08:34.180 --> 01:08:35.130
Look at this.

01:08:35.180 --> 01:08:37.130
The doll?

01:08:37.180 --> 01:08:38.130
What about it?

01:08:38.180 --> 01:08:42.130
Laverne, this is going to save your life.

01:08:42.180 --> 01:08:45.130
But it's just a doll.

01:08:45.180 --> 01:08:46.130
Yes.

01:08:46.180 --> 01:08:50.130
And I'm going to put the handkerchief around the doll.

01:08:50.180 --> 01:08:51.180
Like this.

01:08:52.180 --> 01:08:54.130
And?

01:08:54.180 --> 01:08:57.130
How does that help, Anderson?

01:08:57.180 --> 01:09:03.130
Laverne, it means I can do something to Mr. Andrews.

01:09:03.180 --> 01:09:05.130
Anytime I want.

01:09:05.180 --> 01:09:07.130
What do you mean?

01:09:07.180 --> 01:09:09.130
Do something.

01:09:09.180 --> 01:09:11.130
Watch.

01:09:11.180 --> 01:09:16.130
First of all, I just put this needle into the doll.

01:09:16.180 --> 01:09:19.130
Like this?

01:09:19.180 --> 01:09:24.130
And Mr. Andrews is going to get a sharp pain.

01:09:24.180 --> 01:09:25.130
Right there.

01:09:25.180 --> 01:09:29.130
In his chest.

01:09:29.180 --> 01:09:32.130
Mother, do you know where to...

01:09:32.180 --> 01:09:35.130
What's the matter, Mr. Andrews?

01:09:35.180 --> 01:09:36.130
Nothing.

01:09:36.180 --> 01:09:37.130
I just...

01:09:37.180 --> 01:09:39.130
You okay?

01:09:39.180 --> 01:09:41.130
Sit down for a moment.

01:09:41.180 --> 01:09:45.130
I just got a pain here in my chest.

01:09:45.180 --> 01:09:46.130
It's okay.

01:09:46.180 --> 01:09:47.130
It's gone now.

01:09:47.180 --> 01:09:52.130
Mr. Andrews, when are you going to take my advice and stop drinking so much bourbon?

01:09:52.180 --> 01:09:54.130
Never, Martha, never.

01:09:54.180 --> 01:09:57.130
I like bourbon too much to ever stop.

01:09:57.180 --> 01:10:19.130
A few weeks later, the wedding took place.

01:10:19.180 --> 01:10:24.130
After the church service, there was a big party in Andrews' house in Canal Street.

01:10:24.180 --> 01:10:29.130
Laverne was sick with worry.

01:10:29.180 --> 01:10:32.130
Congratulations, Laverne, and good luck.

01:10:32.180 --> 01:10:34.130
Thanks.

01:10:34.180 --> 01:10:40.130
My God, she needs all the luck she can get.

01:10:40.180 --> 01:10:41.130
Hello, Marie.

01:10:41.180 --> 01:10:43.130
Hi, Margaret.

01:10:43.180 --> 01:10:44.130
How are you feeling?

01:10:44.180 --> 01:10:45.130
Terrible.

01:10:45.180 --> 01:10:48.130
But there is some good news.

01:10:48.180 --> 01:10:52.130
Andrews has already paid off Henry's debts to all the banks in town.

01:10:52.180 --> 01:10:53.130
That's wonderful.

01:10:53.180 --> 01:10:54.130
Marie.

01:10:54.180 --> 01:10:55.130
Hi, Laverne.

01:10:55.180 --> 01:10:56.130
You look lovely.

01:10:56.180 --> 01:10:58.130
Marie, what's going on?

01:10:58.180 --> 01:11:00.130
You said everything would be okay.

01:11:00.180 --> 01:11:03.130
Well, it doesn't feel okay to me.

01:11:03.180 --> 01:11:04.130
Don't panic.

01:11:04.180 --> 01:11:09.130
It would be okay, I promise.

01:11:09.180 --> 01:11:15.130
Ladies and gentlemen, please raise your glasses for a toast to George and Laverne.

01:11:15.180 --> 01:11:18.180
George and Laverne.

01:11:19.180 --> 01:11:28.130
Marie, are you?

01:11:28.180 --> 01:11:30.130
Oh, where's Marie?

01:11:30.180 --> 01:11:33.130
Laverne, did you see where Marie went?

01:11:33.180 --> 01:11:34.130
No.

01:11:34.180 --> 01:11:38.130
No one knew where Marie was, but she was nearby.

01:11:38.180 --> 01:11:43.130
And she had the doll covered with the blood-stained silk handkerchief.

01:11:43.180 --> 01:11:46.130
And she had a sharp needle.

01:11:46.180 --> 01:11:49.130
Dumb balla widow.

01:11:49.180 --> 01:11:52.130
Hear my words.

01:11:52.180 --> 01:11:58.180
Now, ladies and gentlemen, pray silence for the groom, Mr. George Andrews.

01:12:06.180 --> 01:12:07.130
Thank you.

01:12:07.180 --> 01:12:08.130
Thank you very much.

01:12:08.180 --> 01:12:11.130
Well, I'm so glad that you could all come to my wedding.

01:12:11.180 --> 01:12:15.130
And I hope the food and drink are to your satisfaction.

01:12:15.180 --> 01:12:17.130
Well, they should be.

01:12:17.180 --> 01:12:20.180
This whole thing costs me a huge pile of money.

01:12:26.180 --> 01:12:28.130
And let's be honest.

01:12:28.180 --> 01:12:31.130
Money is what this is all about.

01:12:31.180 --> 01:12:33.130
I work hard all my life.

01:12:33.180 --> 01:12:35.130
I mean a lot of money.

01:12:35.180 --> 01:12:37.130
I mean a lot of money.

01:12:37.180 --> 01:12:40.130
And now I get my reward.

01:12:40.180 --> 01:12:42.130
I have a beautiful young bride and...

01:12:42.180 --> 01:12:44.130
Dumb balla widow.

01:12:44.180 --> 01:12:46.130
I...

01:12:46.180 --> 01:12:48.130
What was that?

01:12:48.180 --> 01:12:50.130
What was what, George?

01:12:50.180 --> 01:12:53.130
Voice kinda spooky voice.

01:12:53.180 --> 01:12:56.130
Dumb balla widow.

01:12:56.180 --> 01:12:57.130
That is again.

01:12:57.180 --> 01:12:59.130
Didn't anyone hear it?

01:12:59.180 --> 01:13:02.130
No one heard anything.

01:13:02.180 --> 01:13:04.130
You didn't hear a voice.

01:13:04.180 --> 01:13:06.130
Saying something foreign?

01:13:06.180 --> 01:13:07.130
No, George.

01:13:07.180 --> 01:13:10.180
The only voice we can hear is yours.

01:13:11.180 --> 01:13:13.130
Right.

01:13:13.180 --> 01:13:15.130
Well, as I was saying,

01:13:15.180 --> 01:13:18.130
I have a beautiful young bride.

01:13:18.180 --> 01:13:20.180
Dumb balla widow.

01:13:23.180 --> 01:13:25.130
George, you're right.

01:13:25.180 --> 01:13:27.130
Of course I'm all right.

01:13:27.180 --> 01:13:29.130
It's my wedding day.

01:13:29.180 --> 01:13:31.130
What was I saying?

01:13:31.180 --> 01:13:35.130
I have always believed that what you...

01:13:35.180 --> 01:13:36.130
You get what?

01:13:36.180 --> 01:13:38.180
Dumb balla widow.

01:13:39.180 --> 01:13:41.130
George, sit down, man.

01:13:41.180 --> 01:13:42.130
You look terrible.

01:13:42.180 --> 01:13:44.130
Leave me alone, damn you.

01:13:44.180 --> 01:13:48.130
This is my wedding party, which I have paid for.

01:13:48.180 --> 01:13:53.130
Most people would expect the bride's father to pay for all this, but he didn't.

01:13:53.180 --> 01:13:55.180
Dumb balla widow.

01:13:59.180 --> 01:14:01.130
Get out the way.

01:14:01.180 --> 01:14:03.130
Let him get some air.

01:14:03.180 --> 01:14:05.180
Oh, my God!

01:14:06.180 --> 01:14:08.130
George was dead.

01:14:08.180 --> 01:14:11.130
The doctor said he had a heart attack.

01:14:11.180 --> 01:14:15.130
All of her was a widow before her wedding day was over.

01:14:15.180 --> 01:14:21.130
But I guess inheriting $100,000 of charges money helped her to recover quickly.

01:14:21.180 --> 01:14:23.130
And Marie?

01:14:23.180 --> 01:14:29.180
Well, she was most definitely the queen in New Orleans.
